  "worked_solution": "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y-x}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\ny-x>0\\,\u27f9\\,y>x.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100214/assets/c78759bc8f9926159065129e00c1fc8b4341ab83ad40cc87e67a2e82b3e6b089.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or equal to zero. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,\u27f9\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2},$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100214/assets/bdf02ff330f256327d0c9f1147c388b25184610b36ea7ab77bb2c570ec3be19a.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100214/assets/0d3180a60e33c05e904c8fc00fdec4197510198fef3de3fa8c7b3549609205f6.png)",
